\section*{Split tangents on rationally connected projective manifolds}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Both summands of a specified two-part tangent splitting on a rationally connected projective complex manifold are claimed to be integrable, yielding a compatible product decomposition. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 052, 2026-09-23): A formal diagonal solution separates derivatives through the two holomorphic projectors. Positive normal-bundle section counts force the formal projective ratios to be rational, and tangency contracts the exceptional curves of a resolution, giving a global holomorphic map whose factor directions certify integrability.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 052, 2026-09-23): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 052, the scope page documents the following selections. One selected statement splits the universal cover assuming integrability of both supplied tangent summands. Another establishes that integrability for rationally connected projective manifolds; its compatible-product consequence is not itself that selected statement. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. It does not establish formal coverage of the complete problem clause. Comparator files: KahlerSplitting.lean, SplitTangentIntegrability.lean. Declarations: OAI.UniversalCoverSplitting.main, OAI.SplitTangent.integrability\_main.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

